<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">British India</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> refers to the territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> ruled directly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, first through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and from 1858 by the crown. It expanded by conquest and annexation, absorbing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> after the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> wars of the 1840s. Its northwestern frontier was the focus of rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-india.jpg" alt="British India" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elisha Kirkall (c. 1682–1742) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_William,_Calcutta,_1735.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1608 the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> issued a firman to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> to establish a small trading settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>, which became its first headquarters. The company, incorporated on 31 December 1600, rented a small outpost at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> in 1639. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, ceded to the British Crown by Portugal as part of the wedding dowry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a> in 1661, was granted to the company to be held in trust for the Crown. In eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Job Charnock became tenant of three small villages, later renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, in 1686.</p><p>By the mid-18th century the three principal settlements had become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> presidencies, each administered by a governor. This was not yet territorial expansion: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was not colonised in this period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>'s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757 and the defeat of the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764, the company obtained the Diwani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, the right to administer and collect land revenue. By 1773 it had obtained the Nizamat, and with it full practical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the expanded Bengal Presidency. Portions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> were annexed to the Madras Presidency after 1792, and more after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> in 1799. By the mid-19th century, after the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-maratha-wars">Anglo-Maratha Wars</a> and the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-mysore-wars">Anglo-Mysore Wars</a>, the company had become the paramount power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. The Punjab Province was established in 1849 from territories captured in the Anglo-Sikh Wars.</p><p>After the rebellion of 1857 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1858">Government of India Act 1858</a> ended company rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was directly ruled as a colonial possession of the United Kingdom, officially known as the Indian Empire after 1876. British India consisted of the regions directly administered by the British, beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, whose rulers kept a measure of internal autonomy in exchange for recognising British suzerainty. In 1910 British India covered about 54 per cent of the area and included over 77 per cent of the population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> was part of British India from 1824 to 1937, when it became a separate colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> was partitioned in 1905 and reunited in 1912, the year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> became the capital. At independence in 1947 there were 17 provinces and officially 565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>. Eleven provinces joined India and three joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, Bengal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> were partitioned between them.</p><p>The provinces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were replaced by redrawn states and union territories in 1950. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>, a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> in 1956 and became the independent nation of Bangladesh in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Presidencies and provinces of British India” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>British India</b> was a predominantly rural society of peasant cultivators, organised by caste, religion and region, with Hindus forming the majority and Muslims the largest minority, alongside Sikhs, Christians, Jains, Parsis and tribal peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Company ruled annexed lands directly as <b>British India</b> and the rest through subsidiary alliances with the princely states, from Hyderabad in 1798 to Bahawalpur in 1833.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Islamic Republic of Pakistan, proclaimed in 1956 in the state carved from <b>British India</b> in 1947, lost East Pakistan as Bangladesh in 1971, answered India with nuclear weapons in 1998 and has swung between generals and elected governments, a middle power of 241 million people tied to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithvi Narayan Shah called his kingdom ‘a yam between two boulders’, squeezed between China and <b>British India</b>, a phrase still quoted in debates on Nepal’s foreign policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Burmese expansion into Arakan, Assam and Manipur brought the kingdom into conflict with <b>British India</b>, and the resulting Anglo-Burmese wars led to the incorporation of Burma into the British Empire, with consequences that shaped the borders and ethnic politics of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The subjugation of the Central Asian khanates of Bukhara (1868), Khiva (1873) and Kokand (1876) brought the frontier to the borders of Afghanistan and <b>British India</b>, and generated the 'Great Game' rivalry with Britain that governed Central Asian political geography until the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
